WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.660
This isn't computer animation

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.760
or a trick of the light.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.700
A wood-carved city
actually just popped out

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.991
of that chunk of a tree,

00:00:13.991 --> 00:00:15.910
and it can pop right back in!

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.540
So what's going on?

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.580
Artist Uli Kirchler works
with a totally unexpected

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.610
form of art that requires
a tough-to-navigate canvas.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:29.440
He uses burls, these bulbous
and kind of gross growths

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.803
found on the side of trees.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.460
Look inside them and you'll
see amazing swirling patterns.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.090
But when Uli looks inside,

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.290
he sees an opportunity
to create a pop-up city

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.380
that could take him hours
to meticulously carve

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.370
but that he can play with for a lifetime.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.440
Uli's pieces appear right before your eyes

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.510
but also disappear in an instant.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.610
That's because the carvings

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.990
work like an extendable telescope.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.320
So, how exactly does he carve

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.720
such intricate castles into a tree trunk?

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.790
And how exactly do they work?

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.495
He chooses to use burls

00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:06.837
because each of them have a
natural edge that's unique,

00:01:06.837 --> 00:01:09.253
making each piece he makes one of a kind.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.250
Uli carves into those natural edges

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.410
using different types of saws.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.173
He does most of his
cutting with a scroll saw.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.410
The trick to getting his pieces just right

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.960
is to cut into the wood at a slight angle.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.190
This way, the pieces
are able to move around

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.970
without flying right out.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.092
This also means each level of the castle

00:01:29.092 --> 00:01:31.300
will fit inside the one underneath it,

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.370
creating a telescopic effect.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.270
The castles will be able to expand out

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.610
and collapse back into each other.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.880
Uli Kirchler: I have to be very careful,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.780
especially if it's a bigger piece.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.370
And, often, I stand at my scroll saw,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.280
I feel my heart pounding,
knowing I cannot mess up;

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.530
otherwise, I'm risking to lose the piece.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:51.740
Narrator: He maps out where he'll place

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.090
all the hidden doors and windows
before he starts cutting.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:58.600
Then, he takes each individual
level of the castle out

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.540
and drills holes where
all the details will go.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.600
This way, he can place them
directly under the scroll saw

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.330
in order to make perfect cuts.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.175
Uli: Since I'm a designer as well,

00:02:11.175 --> 00:02:13.441
there has to be a certain distance

00:02:13.441 --> 00:02:15.282
between the different sections.

00:02:15.282 --> 00:02:19.370
So, I intuitively have
to guess that angle.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.480
Narrator: The hardest part
of Uli's process, though?

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.610
Getting the angles just right.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.720
Because the density of the
raw wood he uses differs,

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.310
he has to approach each piece differently.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.090
Getting those angles right every time

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.313
has taken him years of practice.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.360
Uli: Two decades and more of scroll-sawing

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.360
allowed me to refine a
technique and push it further.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.440
It totally changed my life.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.520
Narrator: After everything is cut,

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.250
he adds the final finishing
touches and sands the piece.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.608
Then, it's completed with finishing oil.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.260
For the past couple of years,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.940
Uli has been selling his castles

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.480
at the Portland Saturday
Market every weekend.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.800
His favorite part?

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.603
Watching people react to his work.

00:03:13.656 --> 00:03:14.935
(snaps)

00:03:14.935 --> 00:03:18.382
(laughing)
Shopper: Whoa!

00:03:18.382 --> 00:03:20.223
That is cool!

00:03:20.223 --> 00:03:21.777
Shopper: OK, can you
do that one more time?

00:03:21.777 --> 00:03:22.735
(laughing)

